  "corrected_content": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-161659",
    "knowledge_point_id": "fb891682-26ae-541c-a92d-a79756d1714e",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Volume of a Solid Given in Set Notation",
    "problem": "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le y\\le 2z}.\n$$\n\nInterpret the unchanged inequalities in real coordinates. The square root requires $2-x^2\\ge0$, so the actual nonempty $x$ projection is $[-\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2]$. Find the volume using this real domain.",
    "worked_solution": "The inequalities defining $R$ are retained exactly. In real coordinates, $\\sqrt{2-x^2}$ is defined only for $|x|\\le\\sqrt2$. These values also satisfy the printed nominal bound $-2\\le x\\le2$. Thus the actual real solid is equivalently\n\n$$R=\\{(x,y,z):-\\sqrt2\\le x\\le\\sqrt2,\\ 0\\le z\\le\\sqrt{2-x^2},\\ 0\\le y\\le2z\\}.$$\n\nIts volume is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nV&=\\int_{-\\sqrt2}^{\\sqrt2}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2-x^2}}\\int_0^{2z}1\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-\\sqrt2}^{\\sqrt2}\\int_0^{\\sqrt{2-x^2}}2z\\,dz\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-\\sqrt2}^{\\sqrt2}(2-x^2)\\,dx\n=\\left[2x-\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_{-\\sqrt2}^{\\sqrt2}\n=\\frac{8\\sqrt2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe original source accepted $8/3$. That value is the ordinary signed polynomial integral $\\int_{-2}^{2}(2-x^2)\\,dx$. It does not give the volume of this real solid: the source square-root bounds are undefined for $\\sqrt2<|x|\\le2$. All five original choices are retained as alternatives, and $8\\sqrt2/3$ is added as the correct study answer.",
